text:
carol: Heads up — hitting a nasty combo in prod: streaming responses + Polly retries + HttpClient disposal. If a retry fires after we partially read the response body we get connection errors / Broken pipe. Anyone seen a safe pattern for streaming + retries?

alice: Yep. This is super common. Short version: once you start streaming (ResponseHeadersRead) the body stream is one-time. Polly retries happen before or after that; if after, there's nothing to retry.

bob: Suggested approach: either buffer the response before you let Polly decide, or restrict Polly to only retry BEFORE you start consuming the body. Example: use ExecuteAsync to run the request send and response headers (retryable), then read body separately.

carol: Any code examples?

bob: Sure — rough pattern:

```csharp
// create client once (IHttpClientFactory)
var req = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url);
// only read headers for retry logic
var response = await policy.ExecuteAsync(() =&gt;
    client.SendAsync(req, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead, ct));

// check status and then read/stream content (no more retries)
response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
using var stream = await response.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
await ProcessStreamAsync(stream, ct);
```

Key is that Polly wraps the SendAsync that gets headers; once you start reading you shouldn't let the same policy try again.

alice: If you need full end-to-end retry (including body), you must buffer the body so it's replayable. Two options: ReadAsByteArrayAsync (memory) or copy to a temp file/stream and rewind. Memory is easiest for small payloads.

```csharp
// buffer example for retryable semantics
var bytes = await response.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync(ct);
// process from bytes[0..] and you can re-create ByteArrayContent for retries
var replayContent = new ByteArrayContent(bytes);
```

dave: Also fyi — DO NOT dispose HttpClient per request. Use IHttpClientFactory or a singleton HttpClient. Disposing often causes socket exhaustion / TIME_WAIT and kills keep-alive benefits. If you're getting transient connection resets look at SocketsHttpHandler settings: PooledConnectionLifetime and PooledConnectionIdleTimeout.

carol: We're already using IHttpClientFactory, but we create HttpRequestMessage per call and we dispose response after streaming. Is disposing response a problem?

bob: Disposing HttpResponseMessage is correct after you're done. The problem is starting a read then a retry on the same logical operation. Also watch for HttpCompletionOption: ResponseContentRead buffers whole content internally — ResponseHeadersRead gives a live stream.

carol: We're seeing occasional hangs where CopyToAsync seems to block forever then connection reset. Any tips for large streaming?

alice: A few things to check:
- Use cancellation tokens with timeouts and cancel the read if it's stuck.
- Set MaxConnectionsPerServer higher if you have many concurrent streams.
- If behind a proxy/load balancer it may close idle connections — consider smaller chunk timeouts.

dave: Also watch stream backpressure: if the app isn't draining the stream fast enough the server may close. Consider applying smaller read buffer sizes or piping to disk.

carol: What about Polly config? Currently we do WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt =&gt; TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)). Could retries be triggering mid-stream?

bob: That policy will try on exceptions thrown inside ExecuteAsync. If you wrap the whole stream processing in ExecuteAsync and the stream throws later, it will retry but you won't have the original body to read. So scope the policy narrowly around the send. e.g., policy.ExecuteAsync(() =&gt; client.SendAsync(...)) then separate processing outside the policy.

alice: If you really need to retry entire operation including parse stage, buffer (file/memory) first. Also simpler: retry only for idempotent methods (GET) and only when you haven't started reading.

ci-bot: deploy-bot: noticed 2 retry-related errors from service-x in the last hour.

carol: Thanks all. Plan: change Polly scope to only SendAsync, try buffering for the problematic endpoints, add cancellation tokens and bump MaxConnectionsPerServer. I'll post perf numbers after testing. :thumbsup:

bob: sg. ping me if you want a patch review for the client code.

carol: Will do. Cheers!
